.TH EASYWALL-CORE 1 "2026-08-16" "easywall" "easywall manual"
.SH NAME
easywall\-core \- the privileged half of easywall, and its console controls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B easywall\-core
[\fB\-config\fR \fIpath\fR]
.br
.B easywall\-core
\fIcommand\fR [\fB\-config\fR \fIpath\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B easywall\-core
runs as root, speaks netlink, and owns the nftables table
.IR "inet easywall" .
It is normally started by systemd and reached over a Unix socket by
.BR easywall\-web (1),
which holds no privilege of its own.
.PP
At startup it puts the last confirmed rule set back into the kernel. nftables
rules do not survive a reboot, and without this the machine would come up
unfiltered until somebody opened the web interface and applied. The restore has
no acceptance window: the rule set being restored has already survived one.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Report whether the firewall is enforcing, whether an acceptance window is open,
and when rules were last applied. Exits 0 when the firewall is enforcing or when
panic mode says it was meant not to be, and 2 when the machine is simply not
filtering \- so it can be used from a monitoring check.
.TP
.B panic
Take the firewall down and record that it was deliberate. The machine is
unfiltered immediately and stays unfiltered across a restart. This is the way
back into a machine your own rules have shut you out of.
.PP
.B panic
waits for the nftables lock, which an in-flight
.B apply
holds while its custom-rules syntax check runs. If the firewall appears to be
hung, this is probably the wait. It is not hung.
.TP
.B resume
End panic mode and put the stored rules back.
.PP
Exits 0 even when no daemon is running. In that case
.B resume
clears the panic marker and stops; restoring the rules is the daemon's job and it
does it on start. To complete the recovery, start the service:
.B systemctl start easywall-core
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-config " " \fIpath\fR
Configuration file. Default
.IR /etc/easywall/easywall.toml .
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-write\-config " " \fIpath\fR
Write a commented default configuration to
.I path
and exit. Never overwrites an existing file.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload
.RI [ firewall ],
.RI [ acceptance ],
.RI [ ipv6 ]
and
.RI [ docker ]
without dropping the socket. Paths stay bound to what the daemon started with. A
file that does not parse is refused and the running configuration is kept.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/easywall/easywall.toml
Core configuration.
.TP
.I /var/lib/easywall/rules.json
The three rule sets: current, staged, backup.
.TP
.I /var/lib/easywall/panic
Present while panic mode is engaged. Its presence stops the startup restore.
While it exists, an
.B apply
is refused and an acceptance rollback does not run. The acceptance window's
automatic undo is inactive in panic mode.
.TP
.I /var/log/easywall/audit.log
One JSON object per line: what changed, when, and by whom.
.TP
.I /run/easywall/core.sock
The control socket, root:easywall 0660.
.SH SHUTDOWN
Stopping the daemon waits for a startup restore that may still be writing to the
kernel, and can take up to about thirty seconds. systemd's default
.B TimeoutStopSec
is 90 seconds and provides room for this.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed. The reason is on standard error.
.TP
.B 2
.B status
only: the machine is not filtering and panic mode is not engaged.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR easywall\-web (1),
.BR nft (8)
.PP
https://easywall\-project.org
